WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:05.600
Hello everyone.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.580
Welcome to the Langfocus channel, and my name
is Po-ru.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.190
“Why did English become the international
language?”

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.890
- That is the question we're going to answer today.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.210
English is the de facto global lingua franca.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:21.740
It is the language of global commerce, the
main language of international diplomacy,

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:27.150
the language of air traffic control, and the majority of of academic journals.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.150
 

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.570
Maybe most importantly for us, it's the most
common language on the internet, and it's

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.690
the language that international travellers
with different native languages use to communicate

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:38.829
(the very definition of a lingua franca).

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:41.000
But how did English reach this kind of status?

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.960
Well, it's partly because of the United States
of America's role as a global superpower.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.069
But the scene was set earlier than that.

00:00:48.069 --> 00:00:53.239
In the 16th century England started to establish
overseas colonies, and by the end of the 18th

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:57.549
century the British Empire had grown quite
extensive, with colonies in a few parts of

00:00:57.549 --> 00:01:02.579
India and Africa, in the Caribbean, Canada,
and what was to become the United States.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.020
The British were not the only ones establishing
colonies, of course.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.590
Much of present day Canada and the US was
part of New France, a large area that had

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.460
been colonized by the French.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.110
But these areas were lost to the British during
the 7 years war.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:20.210
Much of what is now the southern and western
United States was colonized by the Spanish,

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:26.049
but these areas were taken in wars with Spain
and later Mexico.

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:30.680
The Dutch had also colonized the area of New
Netherland, but it was lost to the British

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.450
in the late 17th century.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:38.050
There were also non-British immigrants to
the British colonies, for example German speaking

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.050
immigrants.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:43.970
For a long part of the USA's history, German
was its second most widely spoken language.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:48.220
It declined in use during the first world
war, because of the stigma of being associated

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.640
with Germany.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.299
So while there were numerous languages spoken
by settlers, English became the dominant language

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:55.509
in the United States.

00:01:55.509 --> 00:01:56.509
*****

00:01:56.509 --> 00:01:59.859
The British kept on establishing colonies
after American independence, including much

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:05.149
larger areas of the Indian subcontinent and
Africa, Australia and New Zealand, and after

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:06.990
the first world war – in the Middle East.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.539
including colonies on the Indian subcontinent,
in Africa, Australia and New Zealand, and

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:09.539
after the first world war in the Middle East.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:13.510
In 1922 the British Empire covered almost
a quarter of the earth, and about 1/5 of the

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.510
world's population.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.440
Some of these countries became populated by
English settlers, and these are the countries

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.270
where English became the dominant language,
replacing many others.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:27.070
Other countries were not populated by permanent
settlers, only by administrative, military,

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:28.800
and commercial personnel.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.000
In some of these countries, English did not
become the single dominant language, but became

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.540
a prestige language and a convenient lingua
franca that could facilitate communication

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.430
between linguistically diverse populations.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.900
English was not the only colonial language
gaining a foothold throughout the world.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.870
But along with Spanish, and French it was
one of the main ones, and its hard to imagine

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.070
a language other than one of these becoming
the global lingua franca.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:57.140
Through the 19th century it was French, but
English began to surpass French in the 20th

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.140
century.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.430
By the time of the First World War, the United
States had become a strong economic power,

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.990
and its role in the war increased its political
influence in Europe.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:10.750
After the war, the Treaty of Versailles was
written not only in French, but also in English.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.900
This signalled the start of English as a language
of diplomacy.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.430
But it was after WWII when English really
began to develop into the main global lingua

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.840
franca as the United States developed more
fully into a world superpower.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.950
This is partly because after the war, the
United States' military maintained a presence

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:31.510
in Europe to assist in reconstruction efforts,
which greatly increased its international

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:32.510
influence.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:36.700
And this didn't stop with Europe: American
economic, political, and military influence

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.790
over much of the world has been the reality
for decades.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:44.820
As the United States grew in power, advancements
in technology gave rise to mass communication

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.100
and media.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:50.650
American movies, tv, and music spread throughout
the world, exposing regular people to the

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:51.650
English language.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.650
Rather than being just the lingua franca of
international business people and diplomats,

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.900
you can now stay at a youth hostel and communicate
with most of the other travellers in English.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:04.980
This is largely thanks to mass communication,
as well as English as a second language classes

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.180
now being a part of the school curriculum
in many countries.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:16.470
This is something that increased in response
to the growing importance of English.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.660
One the last remaining obstacles to English
truly becoming the world language was the

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.730
USSR during the Cold War.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:26.440
Russian was the lingua franca in most of the
USSR's member states.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.440
And scientific articles during that time were
written mainly in either English or Russian.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.250
But the decline and ultimate collapse of the
USSR resulted in a decline in Russian as a

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.400
lingua franca and language of science.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.570
Today, over 96% of scientific articles are
written in English.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.970
In the past couple of decades, globalization
has solidified the position of English as

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:51.380
a global language, with people from all over
the world interacting with each other more,

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.530
and relying on the global lingua franca.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.620
On top of that, the internet has accelerated
that trend.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.750
English is the number 1 language online, and
people from all over the world are constantly

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:06.010
exposed to English online, and communicate
via social media with people from all over

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:07.930
the world, mainly in English.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:11.460
All you have to do is take one look at the
comments on this channel, and you'll see that

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.760
English functions as the lingua franca of
much of the internet.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.370
So in short, the reach of the British Empire
brought English to all 4 corners of the globe,

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:22.120
and the rise of America built upon that momentum.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.310
And as the world has gotten smaller due to
technology and globalization, English has

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.320
become a truly global language.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.370
Does everyone speak English well?

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:31.370
No of course not.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:36.580
But on top of the roughly 360 million native
speakers of English, there are over a billion

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.500
who speak it as a second language.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.430
Some people say as many as 2 billion people
around the world speak English, I guess depending

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:46.000
on what level of proficiency we include.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.550
Will English be the international language
forever?

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:51.080
Well, forever is very absolute world.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.360
Nothing lasts forever.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:56.730
But it seems that English will be the international
language for the forseeable future.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.630
Will Mandarin become the international language
since China is growing in influence?

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:01.670
I doubt it.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:06.190
China exists in a bubble, cut off from the
rest of the internet and social media world.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:10.210
They basically have their own separate world
online and their own separate media, and this

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.090
limits the amount of cultural influence China
will have on the rest of the world.

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:18.280
I think that people in China will continue
to learn English to communicate with the rest

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:19.350
of the world.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:23.860
But, if you work at a souvenir shop in a tourist
area, Mandarin might become your daily lingua

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:24.860
franca.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.400
Will Esperanto become the internationl language?

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.400
NOPE.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.400
Shrug

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.400
The question of the day:

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.980
If you're watching this video, you obviously
speak English.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.030
How about the other people you know, and the
people in your area?

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.870
How well do most of them know English?

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:42.080
And native speakers of English, what has been
your experience using English while traveling?

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.540
Have you found it easy to get around using
English?

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.340
Does it depend on the location?

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.230
Let us know in the comments down below.

